// Formative practice quiz for ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/forms-context-and-crud/ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label

::ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label o1 - best query::Which query best finds a Save button in a test?{
=getByRole for button, with the name Save#Correct. It finds the element the way a user does, and checks its accessible name.
~querySelector by a CSS class#Not quite. That breaks when styling changes, and says nothing about accessibility.
~A test id#Not quite. A test id is a last resort, for when no role or text works.
~The third button in the page#Not quite. Position is fragile and meaningless to a user.
}

::ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label o1 - label query::getByLabelText fails to find a field that looks labeled on screen. What is the most likely cause?{
=The label is not programmatically associated with the input#Correct. The test has found a real accessibility bug.
~getByLabelText only works for buttons#Not quite. It is meant for form fields.
~You must use a test id for inputs#Not quite. Label queries are preferred for fields.
~The test runner cannot read labels#Not quite. It reads them through the DOM, as long as they are associated.
}

::ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label o2 - assert absence::You want to assert that an error message is not on the page. Which query should you use?{
=queryByText, then assert it is not in the document#Correct. queryBy returns null when nothing matches, rather than throwing.
~getByText, then assert it is not in the document#Not quite. getBy throws before the assertion runs.
~findByText#Not quite. findBy waits for something to appear, and rejects if it never does.
~No query is needed#Not quite. Without a query, there is nothing to assert about.
}

::ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label o2 - async element::Data loads after a fetch and then appears on screen. Which query should you use?{
=await findByText#Correct. findBy retries until the element appears, or times out.
~getByText#Not quite. It throws immediately, before the data arrives.
~queryByText#Not quite. It returns null immediately and does not wait.
~getByText inside a loop#Not quite. findBy already handles the retrying.
}

::ui-tests-with-testing-library-querying-by-role-and-label o3 - user-event::Why are calls such as user.click written with await?{
=user-event methods are async and simulate a full interaction#Correct. Awaiting them makes the following assertions run after the interaction has finished.
~Because React renders only inside promises#Not quite. React does not need that. It is about user-event.
~To make the test slower on purpose#Not quite. It is not about speed.
~await is optional and changes nothing#Not quite. Skipping it can make assertions run too early.
}
